# JAMES git identity — TEMPLATE
#
# SCOPE (per AGENTS.md R6): JAMES attribution applies ONLY to `feat:` commits
# (Conventional Commits). Other commit types (chore, fix, refactor, docs,
# test, build, ci, perf) keep whoever made the change. See AGENTS.md R6
# for the full policy.
#
# INSTRUCTIONS (read once, then delete this comment block):
# 1. Fill in JAMES_EMAIL with the email JAMES wants on feat: commits.
# 2. Save this file as `.harness/secrets/james-git.env` (drop the .template).
# 3. Apply it to the repo (one-time, persists in .git/config):
#
#      # PowerShell
#      Get-Content .harness\secrets\james-git.env | ForEach-Object {
#        if ($_ -match '^\s*([^#][^=]*)=(.*)$') {
#          $name = $Matches[1].Trim()
#          $value = $Matches[2].Trim()
#          git config --local $name $value
#        }
#      }
#      git config --get user.name     # must print: JAMES
#      git config --get user.email    # must print: <the email you set>
#
# 4. The preflight.ps1 and prepush.ps1 scripts read these via `git config`.
#    prepush.ps1 only enforces JAMES on `feat:` (or `feat!:`) commits —
#    other types pass through with whoever's name is on them.
#
# 5. NEVER commit the filled-in james-git.env file. It's in .gitignore (the
#    canonical .gitignore should already ignore .harness/secrets/, or add it).
#
# Why a separate file: the canonical AI-AGENT-README.md uses redacted placeholders
# (ghp_REDACTED_SEE_PROJECT_CONTEXT, etc.). JAMES's git identity is a personal
# value, not a project secret — it lives here, not in the canonical doc.

JAMES_NAME=JAMES
JAMES_EMAIL=REPLACE_WITH_JAMES_EMAIL@example.com
